1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
*music*

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,400
Well, good morning, church.

3
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:46,560
So grateful for you all to join us on this rainy holiday weekend as we wrap up our series on the book of Jonah.

4
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:52,900
Hopefully you all have been as encouraged and challenged as I have as we've continued this story.

5
00:00:52,900 --> 00:01:00,160
And we said from the beginning that it is a familiar story to many of us, but not as familiar of a text of Scripture.

6
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:06,900
Oftentimes we kind of deal with the story of Jonah and more pop culture descriptions of it,

7
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:12,140
which can miss the nuance of what the text of Scripture is actually inviting us to consider.

8
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:14,760
Because we've said the story is not really about a big fish.

9
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:23,920
It's not really about the Ninevites or even Jonah as much as it is about you and me and how we relate to God,

10
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:29,620
how we understand God's character and his invitation for us to participate in his world.

11
00:01:29,620 --> 00:01:32,680
Everything in the text is upside down and exaggerated.

12
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:40,660
Everyone and everything, including the cows, obey God, except for the Israelite prophet,

13
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:44,300
the one that we would expect to be the obedient one in the text.

14
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:52,560
But he's so blinded by his nationalistic pride and his hatred for his enemies that he cannot see what God is inviting him into.

15
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:55,540
This is kind of where we've been through the last three weeks.

16
00:01:55,540 --> 00:02:02,120
And today as we turn our eyes to chapter four, there's been some, throughout the first three chapters,

17
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:09,220
there's been some question as to whether or not Jonah is learning his lesson or not and what his motives are.

18
00:02:09,220 --> 00:02:15,400
And chapter four is really going to give us the clearest understanding and unpacking of Jonah's sin,

19
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:20,380
of what it is that motivated all of his activity in chapters one through three.

20
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:22,900
He pretty much owns up to it in chapter four.

21
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:33,360
And so today my hope is that even though Jonah's kind of disobedience and sin is almost absurd to us in chapter four,

22
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:40,640
which is kind of the point, it's almost a comedy of errors to get us to realize how we participate with God the same way that Jonah does.

23
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:47,040
My prayer is that we will hear in our own stubborn rebellion, God's tender patience.

24
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:56,080
That we might be able to hear God whispering to us, inviting us to repent in the same way he invites Jonah too.

25
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:59,020
And we will end our gathering with communion.

26
00:02:59,020 --> 00:02:59,760
It's in the back.

27
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:07,660
If you didn't get a chance to grab one of these on the way in, you've got 40 minutes or so to make your way back there.

28
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:08,540
So let's pray.

29
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:21,640
God, as our praise goes up, my prayer is that our walls will come down.

30
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:31,380
There's so many walls that we see in Jonah's life that keep him from surrendered obedience to your invitation.

31
00:03:31,380 --> 00:03:44,060
And when we're honest with ourselves, God, we realize that there are so many walls in our own lives that keep us from genuine surrender and repentance and a receiving of your invitation in our lives.

32
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:48,760
And so, God, today I pray that our walls would come down.

33
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:59,140
Holy Spirit, I pray that you would speak to each one of us where we are at and the ways in which we participate in an invitation from you the same way Jonah does.

34
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:03,780
God, help us to see where our hearts align with Jonah's hearts.

35
00:04:03,780 --> 00:04:15,340
And also, God, help us to see your tender mercy and compassion and patience with Jonah in the midst of his rebellion and sin.

36
00:04:15,340 --> 00:04:24,860
And may we, God, hear for ourselves your invitation, gentle, patient whisper of repentance to us.

37
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:36,480
May we be a room full of people completely abandoned, surrendered to you for your glory, for our good, and for the good of our neighbors.

38
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:38,500
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

39
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:39,220
Amen.

40
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:45,180
Well, we're going to be walking through chapter four, so if you want to follow along with me, I'd love for you to get your Bibles.

41
00:04:45,180 --> 00:04:50,220
And just in preparation for next week, if you are a phone Bible person, good.

42
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:51,300
We're not Amish here.

43
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:53,360
We're not telling you that that's a sin.

44
00:04:53,360 --> 00:05:03,920
But as we go into our next series on a digital detox, I'd encourage you to dust off your paper copies of the Scriptures and bring them with you so that you can kind of hold this in your hand.

45
00:05:03,960 --> 00:05:05,280
I think it's good to do that.

46
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:10,800
So, thought experiment that I would love for you to join me in as we begin.

47
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:18,220
I'd love for you to try and recall the last time that you changed your mind about a strong opinion that you held.

48
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:29,660
And I'm not talking about, like, how early is too early to put up a Christmas tree or whether or not Die Hard is a Christmas movie, right?

49
00:05:29,660 --> 00:05:33,980
Like, I know we have strong opinions about these things.

50
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:36,800
I'm not talking about when's the last time you changed your mind about one of those things.

51
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:42,160
But when's the last time you changed your mind about a strong opinion that you had on a weighty subject?

52
00:05:43,320 --> 00:05:54,960
And my hunch is, it might be as hard for you as it was for me when I was preparing this message to come up with the last time you changed your mind about a strong opinion you had on a significant topic.

53
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:56,620
Which means one of two things.

54
00:05:56,620 --> 00:06:03,140
It means this room is full of people that are extremely wise.

55
00:06:05,980 --> 00:06:07,300
Which is option one.

56
00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:16,400
Option two is that this room is full of people who are slightly more stubborn than we might like to acknowledge or admit.

57
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:26,160
And stubbornness is relatively harmless when it's over things like when we put up our Christmas tree or whether or not Die Hard is a Christmas movie or whether or not Android phones should even exist.

58
00:06:26,160 --> 00:06:35,860
But it becomes far more dangerous when we're stubborn about ultimate things.

59
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:38,380
Like, who God is.

60
00:06:38,380 --> 00:06:40,080
How he works.

61
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:48,120
And what he's inviting us into when he talks about his kingdom coming and his will being done on earth as it is in heaven.

62
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:58,180
Especially when our ideas, which my hunch is that all of our ideas about God, not all of our ideas, but all of us have ideas about God that are slightly misinformed or incorrect.

63
00:06:58,180 --> 00:07:01,260
And this is where we see Jonah in chapter 4.

64
00:07:01,340 --> 00:07:14,220
The author gives us a dialogue interaction between God and Jonah that clearly shows Jonah being foolishly stubborn and wrong in his view about who God is, what he's like, and what he's inviting Jonah to participate in.

65
00:07:16,020 --> 00:07:37,700
And if by now I have stalled long enough for you to finally recall the last time that you changed your opinion on a strong, significant matter, my hunch is that it wasn't because you just lost a debate on Facebook to somebody that was smarter than you.

66
00:07:39,540 --> 00:07:45,660
Rarely do we change our minds because someone smarter than us said something that confronted our ideas.

67
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:55,800
Oftentimes, most of the time, I find in my life that when I change my mind, when I have a strong opinion about a significant topic, it's because somebody drew near to me relationally.

68
00:07:56,980 --> 00:08:06,180
But they invested in hearing my story, in hearing the reasons why I have the opinions that I have, and they've asked challenging questions.

69
00:08:06,180 --> 00:08:10,860
And they've engaged with my ideas and helped me see things from a different point of view.

70
00:08:10,860 --> 00:08:14,320
And this is the role that God plays for Jonah in Jonah chapter 4.

71
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:23,720
The God of the universe who created and sustains Jonah, the very breath in Jonah's lungs that he's using to fight with God,

72
00:08:23,720 --> 00:08:29,280
God very easily could have smacked him in the back of the head and said,

73
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:32,760
you foolish, stubborn, rebellious prophet.

74
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:38,340
Don't you see your error, your bad ideas?

75
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:40,080
But that is not what God does.

76
00:08:41,020 --> 00:08:51,820
God patiently and lovingly draws near to Jonah in relationship and asks him questions aimed at helping him see his flawed perspective.

77
00:08:51,820 --> 00:08:59,240
And so my hope and my prayer is that as we read this text, we will hear the God of the universe doing the same to us,

78
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:09,640
drawing near to us in relationship and asking us questions about our strongly held opinions where we may or may not be smarter than he is.

79
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:11,740
So that's where we're headed.

80
00:09:11,740 --> 00:09:14,220
Let's read the text and see where it takes us.

81
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:17,600
Jonah chapter 4 says,

82
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:20,620
But to Jonah, this seemed very wrong.

83
00:09:20,620 --> 00:09:25,540
So the Ninevites have repented immediately after his very bad sermon, right?

84
00:09:25,540 --> 00:09:29,460
His five-word bad sermon that really didn't mention much about God or repentance or anything.

85
00:09:29,460 --> 00:09:31,220
It was just like, you're going to get destroyed.

86
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:35,100
The Ninevites, the people that you would expect to not repent, repent.

87
00:09:35,100 --> 00:09:36,700
And Jonah's not happy about it.

88
00:09:36,700 --> 00:09:38,860
To Jonah, this seemed very wrong.

89
00:09:38,860 --> 00:09:40,260
And he became angry.

90
00:09:40,260 --> 00:09:43,000
And he prayed to the Lord,

91
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:46,460
Isn't this what I said, Lord, when I was still at home?

92
00:09:46,460 --> 00:09:52,360
This is what I tried to forestall by fleeing to Tarshish.

93
00:09:52,440 --> 00:10:00,160
I knew that you are gracious and compassionate, slow to anger and abounding in love, a God who relents from sending calamity.

94
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:05,540
Now, Lord, take away my life, for it is better for me to die than to live.

95
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:14,400
But the Lord replied, just a gentle question from the God of the universe.

96
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:16,200
Is it right for you to be angry?

97
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:21,720
Jonah had gone out and sat down at a place east of the city.

98
00:10:21,720 --> 00:10:23,300
There he made himself a shelter.

99
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:27,280
He sat in its shade and he waited to see what would happen to the city,

100
00:10:27,280 --> 00:10:31,800
still hoping that God might change his mind and do the thing that he wants God to do.

101
00:10:32,620 --> 00:10:40,840
And then the Lord God provided a leafy plant and he made it grow up over Jonah to give shade for his head to ease his discomfort.

102
00:10:40,840 --> 00:10:44,360
Gentle, kind, patient.

103
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:47,700
And Jonah was very happy about the plant.

104
00:10:47,700 --> 00:10:52,420
But at dawn the next day, God provided a worm, which chewed the plant so that it withered.

105
00:10:52,960 --> 00:11:00,880
And when the sun rose, God provided a scorching east wind and the sun blazed on Jonah's head so that he grew faint and he wanted to die.

106
00:11:00,880 --> 00:11:03,680
And he said, it would be better for me to die than live.

107
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:10,380
But God said to Jonah, is it right for you to be angry about the plant?

108
00:11:10,380 --> 00:11:15,800
It is, he said, and I am so angry that I wish I were dead.

109
00:11:17,060 --> 00:11:23,460
But the Lord said, you have been concerned about this plant that you did not tend or make grow.

110
00:11:23,460 --> 00:11:26,320
It sprang up overnight and it died overnight.

111
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:31,280
And should I not have concern for the great city of Nineveh,

112
00:11:31,280 --> 00:11:36,920
in which there are more than 120,000 people who cannot tell their right hand from their left,

113
00:11:36,920 --> 00:11:40,000
and also many animals?

114
00:11:42,160 --> 00:11:45,540
What a great way to end this book that we've been working through.

115
00:11:45,540 --> 00:11:47,740
The book ends with, what about the cows?

116
00:11:47,740 --> 00:11:50,960
I like it too.

117
00:11:50,960 --> 00:11:56,000
Jonah is here in chapter four performing what I would call, in my expert opinion,

118
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,400
a good old-fashioned temper tantrum.

119
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:00,100
He's furious.

120
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:01,360
Why?

121
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:04,340
Because God did the thing that Jonah knew that he would do.

122
00:12:04,340 --> 00:12:07,540
The last thing on earth that Jonah wanted him to do.

123
00:12:07,540 --> 00:12:10,600
And I know that all of us have witnessed the temper tantrum before.

124
00:12:11,740 --> 00:12:14,920
Likely in the aisle of the grocery store in front of us,

125
00:12:14,920 --> 00:12:18,220
whether it's our kids or the kids in front of us in line.

126
00:12:18,220 --> 00:12:20,180
We've all witnessed a temper tantrum.

127
00:12:20,180 --> 00:12:23,880
And they usually look ridiculous to everyone involved,

128
00:12:23,880 --> 00:12:26,520
except for the one having the tantrum.

129
00:12:26,520 --> 00:12:26,860
Correct?

130
00:12:26,860 --> 00:12:29,380
And this is what we see in Jonah.

131
00:12:29,380 --> 00:12:34,820
This tantrum looks ridiculous to pretty much everyone involved.

132
00:12:34,820 --> 00:12:36,500
Because in the moment of a tantrum,

133
00:12:36,500 --> 00:12:39,940
the person having the tantrum can't see anything past their own perspective.

134
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:44,800
They can't see anything past their own desire.

135
00:12:44,800 --> 00:12:48,560
All they can see is themselves and their opinions.

136
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:53,300
And this is exactly what the author of Jonah wants to expose for us in Jonah's life.

137
00:12:53,300 --> 00:12:55,000
He has passionate opinions.

138
00:12:55,000 --> 00:13:02,060
But these passionate opinions come from a limited perspective that disagrees with God's definition of truth

139
00:13:02,060 --> 00:13:07,360
and causes him to rebel against God's authority over his life.

140
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:13,100
So I want to unpack those two limited perspectives with the rest of the time that we have.

141
00:13:13,100 --> 00:13:17,940
His limited perspective causes him to disagree with God's definition of truth

142
00:13:17,940 --> 00:13:22,020
and rebel against God's authority over his life.

143
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:27,040
So first, Jonah disagrees with God's definition of truth.

144
00:13:27,040 --> 00:13:31,860
Jonah says, I knew you would show compassion to the Ninevites.

145
00:13:31,860 --> 00:13:35,640
Subtext, I knew you would do the wrong thing.

146
00:13:35,640 --> 00:13:38,120
This is what Jonah has in his mind.

147
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:44,680
At the heart of Jonah's rebellion is disagreement with how God chooses to define truth and run his world.

148
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:48,280
And he's not even trying to disguise it.

149
00:13:48,280 --> 00:13:53,780
It's so obvious that he literally quotes Scripture back to God.

150
00:13:53,780 --> 00:13:59,080
Did you guys hear this familiar text in Jonah chapter 4?

151
00:13:59,080 --> 00:14:04,460
I knew that you were gracious and compassionate, slow to anger and abounding in love.

152
00:14:04,460 --> 00:14:07,540
When you heard me read that, did that pop something in your head?

153
00:14:07,540 --> 00:14:08,980
Where else does that text come from?

154
00:14:08,980 --> 00:14:09,560
Does anybody know?

155
00:14:11,560 --> 00:14:12,840
Psalm somewhere, okay.

156
00:14:12,840 --> 00:14:15,900
Exodus 34 is the first time, right?

157
00:14:15,900 --> 00:14:21,920
This is God's self-disclosed definition of himself to the nation of Israel, to Moses,

158
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:24,700
right after their own rebellion, right?

159
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:26,120
Their own idolatry.

160
00:14:26,120 --> 00:14:31,820
God says, I am a God who is compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in steadfast love.

161
00:14:31,820 --> 00:14:35,920
But the interesting thing that we have here is the prophet, the Israelite prophet,

162
00:14:35,920 --> 00:14:43,260
who likely had the text of the Torah memorized, only quotes part of the text back to God.

163
00:14:43,260 --> 00:14:50,280
If you are familiar with Exodus 34 or you've grown up in the church, you've heard it in enough times.

164
00:14:50,280 --> 00:14:52,120
The text goes on.

165
00:14:52,120 --> 00:14:56,240
It says, I know you are gracious and compassionate, God, slow to anger and abounding in love.

166
00:14:56,240 --> 00:14:57,300
Jonah stops.

167
00:14:57,300 --> 00:15:01,120
The next phrase is abounding in love and faithfulness.

168
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:06,560
And that Hebrew word for faithfulness can also be defined as truthfulness.

169
00:15:06,560 --> 00:15:11,920
And so Jonah is not even trying to hide the fact that he disagrees with God's definition of truth.

170
00:15:11,920 --> 00:15:16,900
He says back to God, I know you've told us that you're a God who's compassionate, gracious, slow to anger,

171
00:15:16,900 --> 00:15:18,900
abounding in steadfast love and truthfulness.

172
00:15:18,900 --> 00:15:22,940
But I believe that you are a God who is all of those things except for truthful.

173
00:15:22,940 --> 00:15:27,820
Because I don't agree with the way that you're choosing to define truth.

174
00:15:29,440 --> 00:15:33,300
Instead, I think you're a God who doesn't care about justice, who doesn't care about truth.

175
00:15:33,300 --> 00:15:34,920
Instead, you're a God who repents.

176
00:15:34,920 --> 00:15:38,960
This is where Jonah stops quoting Scripture and adds his own commentary.

177
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:42,980
I guess he thinks because he's a prophet that he gets to add to the text.

178
00:15:42,980 --> 00:15:44,140
And this is what he tries to do.

179
00:15:44,140 --> 00:15:46,100
He says, you're not abounding in love and faithful.

180
00:15:46,100 --> 00:15:50,080
You're abounding in love and you relent from sending calamity.

181
00:15:50,080 --> 00:15:53,700
Some texts have it translated, you repent concerning evil.

182
00:15:53,700 --> 00:15:58,280
Jonah here is calling God out on his definition of truth.

183
00:15:58,280 --> 00:16:02,240
He's saying, you have said that you are true, but I say that you actually don't care about justice.

184
00:16:02,240 --> 00:16:05,180
You don't care about the wickedness of the Ninevites.

185
00:16:05,180 --> 00:16:06,720
Jonah is saying, you aren't truthful.

186
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:07,860
You're inconsistent.

187
00:16:07,860 --> 00:16:10,280
You have to be merciful or just.

188
00:16:10,280 --> 00:16:11,200
You can't be both.

189
00:16:11,200 --> 00:16:15,400
And if you're going to try and be both, I'd rather be dead than live in a world that's run by you.

190
00:16:15,400 --> 00:16:19,300
This is where we see Jonah interacting with God.

191
00:16:19,300 --> 00:16:22,160
Literally, partially quoting Scripture and saying,

192
00:16:22,280 --> 00:16:26,220
I don't agree with the way that you have chosen to define truth.

193
00:16:26,220 --> 00:16:31,340
I'd rather define truth in my own image and from my own perspective.

194
00:16:31,340 --> 00:16:37,280
You hear Jonah saying, I'd rather, if I have to live in a world where you're in charge, I'd rather die.

195
00:16:37,280 --> 00:16:44,060
And ultimately behind that, he's saying, I think the world would be a better place if it was run the way that I think it should be run.

196
00:16:45,780 --> 00:16:54,780
And although this example of Jonah is a bit ridiculous, I think if we're honest and self-reflective, we can admit that we've all had similar conversations with God.

197
00:16:54,780 --> 00:17:06,800
We all have a limited and self-centered perspective that often causes us to question or even redefine truth for ourselves in the face of what God says is true about him.

198
00:17:08,780 --> 00:17:14,960
We want a God who affirms our ideology and confronts the ideology of those we disagree with.

199
00:17:14,960 --> 00:17:21,380
And like Jonah, we partially quote Scripture.

200
00:17:21,380 --> 00:17:35,560
We pick and choose the passages of Scripture that allow us to create a God that is more like us than like the God revealed in the pages of Scripture.

201
00:17:37,960 --> 00:17:42,320
And people on both sides of the religious aisle do it.

202
00:17:42,320 --> 00:17:54,100
The religious zealous people obsessed with absolute truth and morality as they define it love to quote passages about Jesus flipping tables and making whips.

203
00:17:54,100 --> 00:18:03,120
Telling sinners to go and sin no more while conveniently ignoring passages about taking care of the widow and the orphan and the immigrant.

204
00:18:04,220 --> 00:18:11,680
Or passages like how you might want to take the log out of your own eye before you get hot and bothered about the speck in your neighbor's eye.

205
00:18:11,680 --> 00:18:19,860
We quote the Scriptures that make us feel right and others wrong.

206
00:18:19,980 --> 00:18:27,080
And when we come in contact with Scriptures that make us feel a little bit uncomfortable or like maybe we have slightly limited perspective on who God is and what he's like.

207
00:18:27,080 --> 00:18:31,020
We try and contextually explain it away and make it fit our own agenda.

208
00:18:31,020 --> 00:18:35,720
This is what Jonah is doing and this is what we often do.

209
00:18:35,720 --> 00:18:41,700
Progressive proponents of postmodernism where all truth is relative and all paths lead to God.

210
00:18:41,800 --> 00:18:43,720
These people, they love to quote Scripture.

211
00:18:43,720 --> 00:18:52,480
They love to look at the teachings of Jesus and see how Jesus ate with sinners and how he told the Pharisees not to cast the first stone.

212
00:18:52,480 --> 00:19:00,340
But they want to pretend that that same Jesus didn't say the words, I am the way, the truth, and the life.

213
00:19:00,340 --> 00:19:04,680
And no one comes to the Father except through me.

214
00:19:04,680 --> 00:19:10,200
Most of us are far more like Jonah than we'd like to admit.

215
00:19:10,200 --> 00:19:14,240
We disagree with truth as God defines it.

216
00:19:14,580 --> 00:19:21,940
We either want a God who's always truthful according to our definition and rarely merciful unless we're the ones who need mercy.

217
00:19:21,940 --> 00:19:32,560
Or we want a God who's always merciful and less concerned with truth except for when those who define truth differently than us have influence or power.

218
00:19:32,560 --> 00:19:36,560
It becomes an identity issue for us.

219
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:46,720
And what's interesting is that at the beginning of the book in chapter one, we heard the author introduce us to Jonah, son of Amittai.

220
00:19:46,720 --> 00:19:50,560
And Amittai literally means truth.

221
00:19:50,560 --> 00:19:56,420
And so Jonah has this identity, this identity issue at the core of who he is.

222
00:19:56,420 --> 00:19:58,680
I am a person of truthfulness.

223
00:19:58,680 --> 00:20:04,040
And how often does this happen to us where our definition of truth becomes our identity?

224
00:20:04,840 --> 00:20:08,660
It becomes a clothing that we wear.

225
00:20:08,660 --> 00:20:16,420
And whenever we come in contact with God trying to challenge our definition of truth, we can't get past it.

226
00:20:16,420 --> 00:20:20,100
That's why I was so fixated on that, the worship song, let our walls come down.

227
00:20:20,100 --> 00:20:29,420
Because our identity of what we define as right and wrong becomes these walls that we cannot hear God challenging us and convicting us in the midst of our sin.

228
00:20:30,620 --> 00:20:38,880
And it's this disagreement with God's definition of truth that leads God to smack Jonah up the side of the head?

229
00:20:38,880 --> 00:20:41,840
No.

230
00:20:41,840 --> 00:20:47,340
It leads God to tenderly draw near to Jonah and ask one simple question.

231
00:20:47,340 --> 00:20:50,780
Jonah, is it good for you to be angry?

232
00:20:52,480 --> 00:21:00,760
Or to put it another way, a way that we might hear echoes of the Garden of Eden, Jonah, is it good for you to define good and evil for yourself?

233
00:21:00,760 --> 00:21:06,160
This is the question that God is tenderly asking Jonah.

234
00:21:06,160 --> 00:21:11,540
The prophet who has the Torah memorized, who knows what is at stake in the Garden of Eden.

235
00:21:13,140 --> 00:21:21,660
People taking from God his ability to define good and evil and to rule the world in his way and trying to define good and evil for themselves.

236
00:21:21,660 --> 00:21:26,420
And Jonah is doing the same thing that people on repeat have been doing since the beginning.

237
00:21:26,420 --> 00:21:30,140
And God draws near to him, just like he draws near to Adam and Eve.

238
00:21:30,140 --> 00:21:33,520
And just like he draws near to you and I when we are in stubborn rebellion and sin.

239
00:21:33,520 --> 00:21:34,520
And he says, is it good?

240
00:21:35,620 --> 00:21:41,080
Is it really as good as you think it is for you to be able to run the universe the way that you think it should be run?

241
00:21:41,080 --> 00:21:45,300
Will this actually lead to the life that you want?

242
00:21:45,300 --> 00:21:55,680
And as stubborn people often do when their bad intellectual ideas are challenged, Jonah doesn't even interact with God.

243
00:21:55,680 --> 00:21:57,000
He says, talk to the hand.

244
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,160
He's not interested in a dialogue.

245
00:21:59,160 --> 00:22:06,520
And so he leaves to the east side of the city, where I believe we see the second motivation of Jonah's temper tantrum.

246
00:22:06,520 --> 00:22:09,660
The first is he disagrees with God's definition of truth.

247
00:22:09,660 --> 00:22:15,420
And the second is he's rebelling against God's authority over his life.

248
00:22:15,420 --> 00:22:27,740
Not only does he not want God's truth to be truth for him, because he disagrees with God's truth, he doesn't want God to have any authority over his life.

249
00:22:27,740 --> 00:22:32,780
And just like we often do when we disagree with God intellectually, we flee from him relationally.

250
00:22:32,780 --> 00:22:45,040
When we come in contact with a passage of Scripture that challenges our definition of God, how often is it that we actually run away from him relationally?

251
00:22:45,040 --> 00:22:46,300
I was thinking about this this week.

252
00:22:46,300 --> 00:22:51,520
There's this kind of Christian culture thing that's been happening for a long time.

253
00:22:51,640 --> 00:22:57,180
It's kind of exited the vogue of Christian culture, but you may have heard of it deconstruction.

254
00:22:57,180 --> 00:23:00,220
You guys heard this concept of people deconstructing their faith.

255
00:23:00,220 --> 00:23:12,940
And oftentimes at the heart of deconstruction is people go away to college or they get sucked into some ideological argument about why the God of the Bible is not a good God.

256
00:23:12,980 --> 00:23:18,120
And why, you know, it goes against all morality that we define for ourselves.

257
00:23:18,120 --> 00:23:23,920
And so we come to grips with this intellectual idea that causes us to question the way that God defines truth.

258
00:23:23,920 --> 00:23:29,480
And what happens is often because we question God's truthfulness, we flee from him relationally.

259
00:23:31,240 --> 00:23:36,220
And going east, which is what Jonah does, the Scripture says that he goes to the east of the city.

260
00:23:36,220 --> 00:23:46,880
This is a literary motif that has been developed throughout the book of Genesis that represents people moving away from God's presence and ultimately trying to recreate Eden apart from God.

261
00:23:46,880 --> 00:23:52,360
This is when the original recipients would have heard him going east of the city.

262
00:23:52,360 --> 00:23:57,920
They would have heard this pattern that is repeated throughout Genesis from the fall to Cain to Babel to Sodom.

263
00:23:57,920 --> 00:24:05,300
All of these people are moving east from Eden, further and further away from God's design, his good presence.

264
00:24:05,300 --> 00:24:09,240
And we're trying to define and create Eden for ourselves apart from God's presence.

265
00:24:09,240 --> 00:24:17,120
We're trying to define truth for ourselves and we're trying to create a world in which we can rule and reign without God's authority in our lives.

266
00:24:17,940 --> 00:24:23,120
So he goes to the east away from God's presence and he builds for himself a shelter.

267
00:24:23,120 --> 00:24:32,880
The same word here for the tents or the booths that God commanded Israel to live in once a week for the Feast of Tabernacles.

268
00:24:32,880 --> 00:24:44,940
They were made out of leafy trees and myrtle and palm and they were to remind Israel that God is at work seeking to restore Eden even now in the midst of our failure.

269
00:24:45,120 --> 00:24:51,500
And so the Israelites, Jonah being one of these, who had participated in the Feast of Tabernacles his whole life,

270
00:24:51,500 --> 00:25:00,840
he knows about living out in a tent for a week to remind him that God is faithful and that God is at work restoring Eden in the midst of the brokenness of humanity.

271
00:25:00,840 --> 00:25:04,220
And instead, Jonah goes out and he builds himself a tent.

272
00:25:04,220 --> 00:25:08,580
He doesn't want God's authority over his life anymore.

273
00:25:08,580 --> 00:25:12,320
He wants to create authority for himself.

274
00:25:14,520 --> 00:25:18,740
Once again, we are far more like Jonah than we'd like to admit.

275
00:25:18,740 --> 00:25:27,480
When we find ourselves disagreeing with God's definition of truth, we often flee his presence and try and recreate the blessing of Eden apart from the presence of God.

276
00:25:27,480 --> 00:25:34,840
We look for comfort and pleasure from God's creation outside of God's definition of how his creation should be used.

277
00:25:35,320 --> 00:25:41,480
And our limited perspective causes us to rebel against God and his authority over our lives.

278
00:25:41,480 --> 00:25:49,440
We want the blessings of Eden without the presence of God.

279
00:25:50,900 --> 00:25:55,800
which leads God, again, to smack Jonah right upside the back of the head like he deserves.

280
00:25:55,800 --> 00:25:58,060
No.

281
00:25:58,060 --> 00:26:06,240
It leads God to once again tenderly, patiently confront Jonah with a lived parable.

282
00:26:06,800 --> 00:26:12,440
This is like Jesus style parables before Jesus came about and started doing parables.

283
00:26:12,440 --> 00:26:20,400
God puts Jonah in a situation where he has to have a lived experience that can go beyond his intellect and to his heart.

284
00:26:20,400 --> 00:26:28,680
This is God doing exactly what the people who drew near to you relationally that helped you change your mind about a strong opinion that you had.

285
00:26:28,740 --> 00:26:30,140
This is exactly what God is doing here.

286
00:26:30,140 --> 00:26:32,780
He's not, he's no longer engaging Jonah's mind.

287
00:26:32,780 --> 00:26:34,660
He knows that that's not going to work.

288
00:26:34,660 --> 00:26:37,140
He begins to engage Jonah's heart.

289
00:26:37,140 --> 00:26:48,140
And God appoints, or as the NIV translates it, provides a plant to grow up over Jonah to provide shade.

290
00:26:48,140 --> 00:26:52,200
Jonah already had some shade.

291
00:26:52,200 --> 00:26:55,780
He built for himself a tent and God says, hey, I can build a better tent.

292
00:26:56,020 --> 00:26:59,920
I can do it overnight at the snap of a finger or the speaking of a word.

293
00:26:59,920 --> 00:27:01,900
I'm going to bring a plant over top of you.

294
00:27:01,900 --> 00:27:10,480
And the text says, he made this leafy plant grow up over Jonah to give shade for his head, to ease his discomfort.

295
00:27:10,480 --> 00:27:15,140
The thing that I love about this is that word for discomfort can also mean evil.

296
00:27:15,140 --> 00:27:19,840
This is such a sophisticated text of Scripture.

297
00:27:19,840 --> 00:27:22,960
The author is literally, he puts this ambiguous word here.

298
00:27:22,960 --> 00:27:25,760
So we're not actually sure what the leaf is doing.

299
00:27:25,760 --> 00:27:36,020
Is the leaf providing comfort for Jonah or is the leaf confronting Jonah's evil actions of running away from God and planting a garden for himself in the desert?

300
00:27:36,020 --> 00:27:38,620
Or is it doing both?

301
00:27:38,620 --> 00:27:44,440
Which I think what God does here, he draws near to Jonah and he provides comfort for him.

302
00:27:44,440 --> 00:27:52,560
But the comfort that he provides for him is also going to engage his heart with his bad ideas that have lead him to rebellion and evil.

303
00:27:52,560 --> 00:28:00,120
And at last we see our grumpy, self-righteous and disobedient prophet happy for the first time in all of the Scripture.

304
00:28:01,340 --> 00:28:08,080
It says that he is exceedingly glad, he's very happy about this plant that he has received from God.

305
00:28:08,080 --> 00:28:13,360
This undeserved mercy of God on his life makes him extremely joyful.

306
00:28:13,360 --> 00:28:21,440
And the next thing God does is he appoints a worm to eat the plant.

307
00:28:22,140 --> 00:28:30,900
And an east wind that seems to not only have blown the plant, the dead plant away, but it seems to have blown the tent that Jonah created for himself away.

308
00:28:30,900 --> 00:28:37,280
And so now Jonah is sitting east of the city in the desert with no shade, completely uncomfortable.

309
00:28:37,280 --> 00:28:42,160
And it takes two verses for him to go from finally being happy to being miserable.

310
00:28:42,160 --> 00:28:44,480
And yet again, ready for God to kill him.

311
00:28:47,300 --> 00:28:56,460
And this word here, appoint, or as the NIV translates it, provide, is the same word about God providing the fish to swallow Jonah.

312
00:28:56,460 --> 00:29:01,080
God is appointing things to try and get Jonah's attention.

313
00:29:01,080 --> 00:29:07,280
Each time he appoints something after Jonah rebels, after Jonah leaves and does the thing that God told him not to do,

314
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:09,480
God appoints something to try and get his attention.

315
00:29:09,480 --> 00:29:15,880
It's something that looks uncomfortable and difficult, but it is ultimately trying to lead him to life.

316
00:29:16,960 --> 00:29:24,460
And so just a small side point I want to ask us is, is it possible there's some things in our lives that are uncomfortable,

317
00:29:24,460 --> 00:29:31,480
that are difficult, that are challenging, that in some ways look like they're a response to the bad decisions that we've made.

318
00:29:31,480 --> 00:29:38,760
Is it possible that God is appointing some things in your life to try and get your attention and draw you back into relationship with himself?

319
00:29:42,360 --> 00:29:53,040
The God of the universe, who Jonah has disagreed with, ignored, and ran away from, continually and patiently re-engages Jonah in relationship.

320
00:29:53,040 --> 00:30:01,340
And God revisits the same question he asked Jonah earlier, but with a slight twist, no longer engaging his intellect, but instead his emotions.

321
00:30:03,680 --> 00:30:15,040
He says, is it right for Jonah's attention, finally gets Jonah to begin to engage with him.

322
00:30:15,040 --> 00:30:17,760
And Jonah's really mad.

323
00:30:17,760 --> 00:30:20,440
This is the height of his temper tantrum.

324
00:30:20,440 --> 00:30:25,660
He says, of course it is good for me to be mad about this plant, so mad that I wish I were dead.

325
00:30:28,280 --> 00:30:32,620
So, what do you all think about that question?

326
00:30:32,620 --> 00:30:36,340
Is it good for Jonah to be mad about the plant?

327
00:30:36,340 --> 00:30:45,980
Nice try, Peyton.

328
00:30:45,980 --> 00:30:49,280
I appreciate the effort.

329
00:30:49,280 --> 00:30:52,000
I appreciate the boldness to speak out.

330
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:55,240
I actually, I think it's great for Jonah to be mad about the plant.

331
00:30:56,520 --> 00:31:00,320
It's just not good that Jonah's mad about the plant and doesn't care about people.

332
00:31:00,320 --> 00:31:04,300
And this is what God is trying to get his attention on.

333
00:31:04,300 --> 00:31:06,120
You're mad about the plant.

334
00:31:06,120 --> 00:31:10,780
You have pity over the plant that you did not plant and did not water, and that's good.

335
00:31:10,780 --> 00:31:17,960
But if that's good, don't you think it's okay for me to pity an entire city of people created in my image?

336
00:31:20,300 --> 00:31:25,540
And this becomes the question that the author of Jonah wants each of us to ponder.

337
00:31:26,420 --> 00:31:32,700
Not as if we're asked, not as if it were asked to Jonah, but as if it were directed to us.

338
00:31:35,580 --> 00:31:43,220
Even in the face of our disagreement with how God chooses to run his world, with how he allows suffering and pain to continue.

339
00:31:43,220 --> 00:31:53,720
With how seemingly people who rebel against him get blessed, while others who are surrendered to him face the difficulty and suffering of a fallen world.

340
00:31:57,200 --> 00:32:05,320
God tenderly whispers to us, do you think it's okay if I get to decide what it looks like for me to be compassionate and gracious?

341
00:32:05,320 --> 00:32:10,700
Slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness.

342
00:32:11,500 --> 00:32:17,920
Or in other words, is it right for the creator to define truth and rule his world the way he sees fit?

343
00:32:17,920 --> 00:32:23,760
And the next question underneath that becomes for us, are we willing to submit to that?

344
00:32:23,760 --> 00:32:33,960
This is what I think that the ending of Jonah wants us to ponder is, is it good for God to get to rule his world his way?

345
00:32:33,960 --> 00:32:37,740
And are we willing to submit to it?

346
00:32:41,500 --> 00:32:47,180
God tenderly confronts our desires to disagree with his truth and take his throne.

347
00:32:47,180 --> 00:32:50,880
This is the story of, this is how the story of Jonah ends.

348
00:32:50,880 --> 00:32:56,340
God tenderly confronting us when we don't like his definition of truth.

349
00:32:56,340 --> 00:33:01,760
And when we don't want his authority over our life, we don't see him smacking Jonah over the head.

350
00:33:01,760 --> 00:33:07,240
We see him tenderly and compassionately and patiently drawing near to Jonah.

351
00:33:08,020 --> 00:33:13,000
And the theme of, I feel like the theme of this entire series for the past few weeks has been,

352
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:16,960
you and I are far more like Jonah than we realize or like to admit.

353
00:33:16,960 --> 00:33:18,440
We run from God's invitation.

354
00:33:18,440 --> 00:33:23,960
We disagree with his definition of truth and we resist his authority in our lives.

355
00:33:23,960 --> 00:33:31,980
Trying to produce a life with the blessings of Eden apart from the relational fatherly presence of God in our lives.

356
00:33:32,980 --> 00:33:36,300
And I think it's good for us to be confronted with this reality.

357
00:33:36,300 --> 00:33:46,020
But as we end the series, I want to intentionally make a shift where we take our eyes off of ourselves and our own propensity to be like Jonah.

358
00:33:46,020 --> 00:33:56,540
And just for the rest of our time today, I just want us to shift our eyes back to God who patiently and lovingly pursues us.

359
00:33:57,600 --> 00:34:01,620
Pursues Jonah, pursues you, and who pursues me.

360
00:34:01,620 --> 00:34:11,320
Not only are we far more like Jonah than we realize or admit, but God is far more gracious than we typically give him credit for.

361
00:34:11,320 --> 00:34:16,080
I mean, just look at how he treats the Ninevites.

362
00:34:17,820 --> 00:34:24,840
A city who is in complete rebellion, who it says, don't know their left from their right, who are the enemies of God's people.

363
00:34:24,840 --> 00:34:29,340
God shows them compassion and grace.

364
00:34:29,340 --> 00:34:34,760
Just look at how he treats Jonah, the rebellious and self-indulgent prophet.

365
00:34:34,760 --> 00:34:39,040
God, through the book of Jonah, confronts our hypocrisy, our bigotry, and our idolatry.

366
00:34:39,040 --> 00:34:49,120
But he does so tenderly with both whales and worms, all to get our attention so that we might hear his gracious invitation.

367
00:34:49,120 --> 00:34:57,900
Is it right for me, the creator of the universe, to define truth and rule my world as I see fit?

368
00:34:57,900 --> 00:35:00,460
And are you willing to trust that?

369
00:35:05,600 --> 00:35:11,340
Jonah wants a God who is just but not merciful unless he needs mercy.

370
00:35:11,340 --> 00:35:24,200
And others oftentimes want a God who's merciful but not just unless their definition of truth is at stake.

371
00:35:26,400 --> 00:35:36,020
And the God of the Scriptures has this incredible way throughout all of human history of being equally both.

372
00:35:36,020 --> 00:35:40,920
He's not a God of justice or mercy.

373
00:35:40,920 --> 00:35:44,460
He is a God that provides mercy through his justice.

374
00:35:45,540 --> 00:35:52,560
And as we end the gathering, I want us to meditate on this reality by preparing to take communion together.

375
00:35:52,560 --> 00:35:58,680
And as we prepare to take communion together, I want us to meditate on two verses from Jonah chapter 4.

376
00:35:58,680 --> 00:36:05,140
And Jonah's frustration with God refusing to give the Ninevites justice.

377
00:36:05,140 --> 00:36:07,640
He states,

378
00:36:08,540 --> 00:36:22,140
Jonah knows that God has a track record of repenting from sending calamity.

379
00:36:22,140 --> 00:36:29,000
He knows because the first time that God said the verse that Jonah quoted back to him,

380
00:36:29,000 --> 00:36:31,360
God was showing mercy to Jonah's people.

381
00:36:32,160 --> 00:36:43,000
Who had just constructed a golden calf and had a real weird religious service that involved lots of immorality and debauchery and drunkenness.

382
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:50,160
And God was compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness.

383
00:36:50,160 --> 00:36:56,580
Jonah knows that God often withholds his justice and his wrath.

384
00:36:58,440 --> 00:37:05,680
And in God's speech to Jonah at the end of the book, he says that the Ninevites are a people who do not know their right from their left.

385
00:37:05,680 --> 00:37:14,980
And with those two texts in mind, I want to make the argument that this is the gospel.

386
00:37:14,980 --> 00:37:22,100
That each one of us are like the Ninevites who do not know our right from our left.

387
00:37:22,820 --> 00:37:29,480
We have a limited perspective and we want to define good and evil for ourselves apart from God.

388
00:37:29,480 --> 00:37:38,000
And God, who would be just to punish us, instead is eager to repent from sending judgment.

389
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:42,660
And he does so for the Ninevites and for us the same way.

390
00:37:42,660 --> 00:37:47,020
Through the death, burial, resurrection, and ascension of Jesus.

391
00:37:48,120 --> 00:37:49,500
That's what Romans 3 is all about.

392
00:37:49,500 --> 00:37:52,040
Romans 3, verse 25 says,

393
00:37:52,040 --> 00:37:56,260
For God presented Jesus as the sacrifice for sin.

394
00:37:56,260 --> 00:38:03,540
People are made right with God when they believe that Jesus sacrificed his life, shedding his blood.

395
00:38:03,540 --> 00:38:07,840
This sacrifice shows that God was being just.

396
00:38:07,840 --> 00:38:13,340
He was being fair when he chose to hold back punishment for those who had sinned in times past.

397
00:38:13,340 --> 00:38:17,300
Paul has the Ninevites in mind here as he writes this text.

398
00:38:17,300 --> 00:38:21,160
God withheld judgment from the Ninevites because of Jesus.

399
00:38:21,160 --> 00:38:24,400
He withholds judgment for us because of Jesus.

400
00:38:24,400 --> 00:38:28,920
For he was looking ahead and including them in what he would do in the present time.

401
00:38:28,920 --> 00:38:36,780
God did this to demonstrate his righteousness, his mercy and justice perfectly balanced with one another.

402
00:38:36,780 --> 00:38:40,880
Not mercy or justice, but mercy through justice.

403
00:38:42,100 --> 00:38:48,040
God did this to demonstrate his mercy through justice for he himself is fair and just.

404
00:38:48,040 --> 00:38:53,200
He makes sinners right in his sight when they believe in Jesus.

405
00:38:53,200 --> 00:39:02,380
God is eager to repent from calamity in exchange for grace, but it requires that we be eager to repent from our sin and turn to him in faith.

406
00:39:02,520 --> 00:39:03,520
God is eager to repent from the Lord.

407
00:39:03,520 --> 00:39:04,520
God is eager to repent from the Lord.

408
00:39:04,520 --> 00:39:05,520
God is eager to repent from the Lord.

409
00:40:23,520 --> 00:40:28,520
All of the judgment and suffering decided for each of us fell.

410
00:40:28,520 --> 00:40:34,520
The judgment and suffering of the whole world fell on Jesus at the cross.

411
00:40:34,520 --> 00:40:36,520
I love this line.

412
00:40:36,520 --> 00:40:44,520
In reality, God's repenting in the face of man's repentance is Jesus Christ.

413
00:40:44,520 --> 00:40:58,520
God is patient and tender, slow to anger, abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness.

414
00:40:58,520 --> 00:41:03,520
And we see that balance of love and faithfulness at the cross of Jesus.

415
00:41:03,520 --> 00:41:06,520
And so this morning, if you are a follower of Jesus,

416
00:41:06,520 --> 00:41:12,520
I would encourage you to prayerfully consider participating in the bread and cup,

417
00:41:12,520 --> 00:41:18,520
which is a reminder for us, a regular reminder for us,

418
00:41:18,520 --> 00:41:22,520
that all of God's promises find their yes in Jesus,

419
00:41:22,520 --> 00:41:28,520
that all of God's mercy in the old covenant found its justice in Jesus,

420
00:41:28,520 --> 00:41:34,520
that all of God's mercy to us in our rebellion and sin finds its justice in Jesus,

421
00:41:34,520 --> 00:41:41,520
that all of the suffering and eternal consequences that we deserve for our rebellion was placed on Jesus.

422
00:41:41,520 --> 00:41:45,520
And so when we partake in his body and his blood,

423
00:41:45,520 --> 00:41:48,520
we remember that God is compassionate and gracious,

424
00:41:48,520 --> 00:41:53,520
slow to anger, abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness.

425
00:41:53,520 --> 00:41:55,520
God doesn't forsake justice.

426
00:41:55,520 --> 00:42:00,520
He places the justice we deserve onto Jesus.

427
00:42:00,520 --> 00:42:02,520
So if you're a follower of Jesus,

428
00:42:02,520 --> 00:42:06,520
I encourage you to take Christ's body broken for you.

429
00:42:06,520 --> 00:42:21,520
Take Christ's blood shed for you.

430
00:42:21,520 --> 00:42:36,520
As I was thinking about this message, the old hymn came to my mind.

431
00:42:41,520 --> 00:42:46,520
Now it's gone from my mind.

432
00:42:46,520 --> 00:42:51,520
Earnestly, tenderly, Jesus is calling.

433
00:42:51,520 --> 00:42:53,520
Calling for sinners.

434
00:42:53,520 --> 00:42:55,520
Come home.

435
00:42:57,520 --> 00:43:01,520
And so if you're in a spot where you find yourself like Jonah,

436
00:43:01,520 --> 00:43:06,520
either disagreeing with how God chooses to run his world,

437
00:43:06,520 --> 00:43:10,520
or rebelling against his authority over your life,

438
00:43:10,520 --> 00:43:14,520
my prayer is that you would hear an invitation from the Father this morning.

439
00:43:14,520 --> 00:43:16,520
Come home.

440
00:43:18,520 --> 00:43:22,520
Is it good for you to define good and evil for yourself?

441
00:43:22,520 --> 00:43:26,520
Or is the God of the universe who made you and sustains the very breath in your lungs?

442
00:43:26,520 --> 00:43:28,520
Is he worthy to be surrendered to?

443
00:43:33,520 --> 00:43:40,520
And for others of us who find ourselves frustrated and angry at people that we disagree with?

444
00:43:40,520 --> 00:43:45,520
People outside the walls of this church who don't,

445
00:43:45,520 --> 00:43:50,520
who don't believe in God, who don't think highly of the gospel,

446
00:43:50,520 --> 00:43:52,520
who think the Scriptures are foolish.

447
00:43:52,520 --> 00:44:09,520
My question for you is, is anger and disagreement and good arguments intellectually going to do anything to change that person's mind?

448
00:44:09,520 --> 00:44:14,520
Or might God be inviting you to take the tactic that he takes with you?

449
00:44:15,520 --> 00:44:18,520
And might we engage people who think the Scriptures are foolish,

450
00:44:18,520 --> 00:44:21,520
who thinks God is unjust or unkind or doesn't exist.

451
00:44:21,520 --> 00:44:28,520
May the invitation for us be to extend a hand of compassion and love to those people.

452
00:44:28,520 --> 00:44:30,520
To hear their stories.

453
00:44:30,520 --> 00:44:33,520
To ask thoughtful questions.

454
00:44:33,520 --> 00:44:35,520
And to let God be God.

455
00:44:35,520 --> 00:44:43,520
I think so much conflict happens because Christians try and make people believe what they believe.

456
00:44:45,520 --> 00:44:52,520
When in reality, God is just inviting us to relationally engage with people who don't know their right from their left.

457
00:44:52,520 --> 00:44:57,520
And not to judge them or condemn them for that, but to ask them questions.

458
00:44:57,520 --> 00:45:05,520
And give them space to think about whether or not there might be a God who created and sustains the universe.

459
00:45:05,520 --> 00:45:08,520
And whether or not he might be trustworthy for them to surrender their lives to.

460
00:45:12,520 --> 00:45:15,520
So, Father, we just, we pray for people all over this room.

461
00:45:15,520 --> 00:45:21,520
I don't know each person's circumstances, but I'm sure there are people that are in rebellion to you.

462
00:45:21,520 --> 00:45:29,520
That are having conversations with you where they're frustrated and disagree with the way in which you're allowing things to happen in their life.

463
00:45:29,520 --> 00:45:31,520
I pray this morning, God, that they would hear your tender whisper.

464
00:45:34,520 --> 00:45:41,520
And that they would look to the cross and realize that through the cross you are, you have demonstrated yourself worthy to be surrendered to.

465
00:45:41,520 --> 00:45:52,520
And God, for those of us that have had conversations with people that we disagree with recently.

466
00:45:53,520 --> 00:45:57,520
Or we get angry and frustrated and try and convince them that they're wrong and we're right.

467
00:45:57,520 --> 00:46:02,520
God, would you give us your heart for them.

468
00:46:02,520 --> 00:46:09,520
The way we see it demonstrated to Jonah that you are patient and kind.

469
00:46:09,520 --> 00:46:16,520
That you're not intimidated by people's rebellion and disagreement.

470
00:46:18,520 --> 00:46:23,520
God, may we be your hands and your feet to people who disagree with us.

471
00:46:23,520 --> 00:46:32,520
So that your kingdom might be more clearly displayed on earth as it is in heaven for your glory, for our good and for the good of our neighbors.

472
00:46:32,520 --> 00:46:34,520
It's in Jesus name I pray.

473
00:46:34,520 --> 00:46:36,520
Amen.

474
00:46:36,520 --> 00:46:42,520
Prayer tent is available if you would like it.

475
00:46:42,520 --> 00:46:44,520
And go in peace.

476
00:46:44,520 --> 00:46:45,520
Go in peace.

477
00:47:14,520 --> 00:47:17,980
Thank you.
